@base = http://localhost:5127
@alice = 11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111
@bob = 22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222

### 1. Healthy partner: one call, transfer accepted
POST {{base}}/transfers
Content-Type: application/json

{ "source": "{{alice}}", "target": "{{bob}}", "amount": 100 }

### 2. The partner becomes flaky: its next 2 answers are 503
POST {{base}}/partner/mode
Content-Type: application/json

{ "mode": "flaky", "failures": 2 }

### 3. Accepted all the same: two retries (200 ms, then 400 ms) absorb the failures
POST {{base}}/transfers
Content-Type: application/json

{ "source": "{{alice}}", "target": "{{bob}}", "amount": 100 }

### 4. The partner becomes slow (3 s per answer, timeout at 1 s)
POST {{base}}/partner/mode
Content-Type: application/json

{ "mode": "slow" }

### 5. Four timeouts, then the fallback: a small transfer goes through, unverified (≈ 5.5 s)
POST {{base}}/transfers
Content-Type: application/json

{ "source": "{{alice}}", "target": "{{bob}}", "amount": 50 }

### 6. The partner is down
POST {{base}}/partner/mode
Content-Type: application/json

{ "mode": "down" }

### 7. A large transfer cannot do without the check: 503 and Retry-After (the circuit opens on the way)
POST {{base}}/transfers
Content-Type: application/json

{ "source": "{{alice}}", "target": "{{bob}}", "amount": 600 }

### 8. Circuit open: answered at once, the partner is not even called
POST {{base}}/transfers
Content-Type: application/json

{ "source": "{{alice}}", "target": "{{bob}}", "amount": 10 }

### 9. What the strategies did, and how many calls the partner received
GET {{base}}/resilience

### 10. The partner is back; 30 s after the opening, a trial call closes the circuit
POST {{base}}/partner/mode
Content-Type: application/json

{ "mode": "healthy" }

### 11. (after the 30 s break) Accepted, checked: the circuit is closed again
POST {{base}}/transfers
Content-Type: application/json

{ "source": "{{alice}}", "target": "{{bob}}", "amount": 10 }
